You can do this with an **`LD_PRELOAD` shim**: build a tiny shared object that defines `geteuid()` (and optionally `getuid()`), then preload it so your definition is used instead of libc’s.

### 1) Write the override (geteuid_shim.c)
```c
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include <unistd.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <errno.h>

typedef uid_t (*geteuid_fn_t)(void);

uid_t geteuid(void) {
  const char *s = getenv("FAKE_EUID");
  if (s && *s) {
    char *end = NULL;
    errno = 0;
    unsigned long v = strtoul(s, &end, 10);
    if (errno == 0 && end && *end == '\0') return (uid_t)v;
  }

  // Fallback to real geteuid
  static geteuid_fn_t real_geteuid = NULL;
  if (!real_geteuid) {
    real_geteuid = (geteuid_fn_t)dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, "geteuid");
  }
  return real_geteuid ? real_geteuid() : (uid_t)-1;
}
```

### 2) Compile the shared library
```bash
gcc -shared -fPIC -O2 -Wall -Wextra -o libfakegeteuid.so geteuid_shim.c -ldl
```

### 3) Use it
```bash
FAKE_EUID=0 LD_PRELOAD=$PWD/libfakegeteuid.so id -u
FAKE_EUID=1234 LD_PRELOAD=$PWD/libfakegeteuid.so some_program
```

Notes:
- This only affects dynamically linked programs that call `geteuid()` (not statically linked binaries).
- It won’t change kernel-enforced privileges—just what the process *thinks* `geteuid()` returns (useful for testing).
- If you also need to intercept `getuid()`, `getresuid()`, etc., add similarly named functions in the same `.c` file.

If you tell me your OS (Linux/macOS) and whether the target binary is static/dynamic, I can tailor this (macOS uses `DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES` instead of `LD_PRELOAD`).
